# GX Validation Suite Authoring Guide

## Overview of Files Involved

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `configs/<pipeline>_preprod.yaml` + `<pipeline>_prod.yaml` | Enable GX and declare nested columns (config file names vary by pipeline) |
| `gx_suite_definitions/<transform>.ipynb` | Author and generate the expectation suite |
| `src/agoradatatools/great_expectations/gx/expectations/<transform>.json` | Generated suite loaded at runtime |
| `src/agoradatatools/great_expectations/gx/json_schemas/<transform>/` | JSON schemas for nested/link columns |

---

## Prerequisite — Environment Setup

Every step in this guide runs Python, `jupyter`, or the `adt` CLI, so before anything else set up and
activate your environment per `python-env.mdc` (package manager, `dev_config.yaml`, and an editable
`pip install -e ".[dev]"`). That rule owns environment setup for **every** workflow here — Option A
and Option B alike — and all commands below assume the correct environment is active.

---

## Core Principle — Tighten Validations to the Actual Data

Always author expectations against the **latest processed output data** (downloaded from Synapse when available, or freshly generated), and tighten every validation to what is actually present in that data rather than to loose, generic bounds.

- **Use current output data first.** Prefer downloading the latest processed output from Synapse; if using a local file in `staging/`, confirm it is current (or re-download) before deriving any bounds or value sets, so the suite reflects the data as it exists now.
- **Numeric ranges:** use the observed `min` and `max` from the data (e.g. `expect_column_values_to_be_between(column, min_value=<observed_min>, max_value=<observed_max>)`). Do not invent padded or "safe" ranges.
- **Allowed value sets:** for `expect_column_values_to_be_in_set`, enumerate the distinct values actually present in the column. Do not include speculative values that do not appear in the data.
- **List length / item counts:** derive `minItems`/`maxItems` (or `list_length_range`) from the observed minimum and maximum lengths in the data.
- **String patterns:** derive regex and length bounds from the actual values present (see "Inspect the staging file before writing regex expectations").

The goal is a suite that fails loudly if future data drifts outside the range or value set observed today. Compute these bounds programmatically from the downloaded data — do not guess.

```python
# Derive numeric bounds from the data
print(df["my_numeric_col"].min(), df["my_numeric_col"].max())

# Derive the allowed value set from the data
print(sorted(df["my_category_col"].dropna().unique()))

# Derive list-length bounds from the data
lengths = df["my_list_col"].dropna().map(len)
print(lengths.min(), lengths.max())
```

