Before finalizing a change to any GX list in the tables above, prompt the user, e.g.:
> "This changes the `<column>` value set in the `<suite>` GX suite. The parallel filter list
> `<variable>` in the ui_config notebook (`AG-2005_ui_config.rmd` for Agora,
> `MG-402_Create ui_config source file.rmd` for model_ad) should be updated to match — do you want
> to update it too, or should the two intentionally differ?"

#### Adding a NEW value-set expectation — check for a parallel first

Whenever you are about to add a **new** `expect_column_values_to_be_in_set` or
`expect_column_values_to_have_list_members` expectation — **or a new `enum` on a property inside a
nested column's JSON Schema** — that is not already listed in the tables above, first check the
ui_config notebooks for an existing parallel filter-value list covering the same vocabulary
(`AG-2005_ui_config.rmd` for Agora datasets, `MG-402_Create ui_config source file.rmd` for model_ad
datasets). Match on the column/filter meaning, then compare the actual value sets.

If you find a parallel list, prompt the user to confirm, e.g.:
> "The new `<column>` value set in the `<suite>` GX suite looks parallel to the `<variable>`
> filter list in `<notebook>`. Should I add this pair to the '<Agora|model_ad> — Parallel lists'
> table in this rule and align the GX value set with the notebook list?"

On confirmation: align the GX value set with the notebook list (or surface any difference for the
user to resolve), **and add a new row to the appropriate "Parallel lists" table above** so the
mapping stays documented. If no parallel list exists, no table update is needed.

### Internal sync — `list_length_in_range` max vs. `list_members` count

When a column has both an `expect_column_values_to_have_list_length_in_range` expectation and an
`expect_column_values_to_have_list_members` expectation (i.e. the list's allowed contents are a
fixed, enumerable set of members), the **maximum** of the length range should equal the number of
allowed members in the `list_members` set — a list can't validly contain more distinct allowed
values than the set defines. Only deviate from this if the user explicitly provides different
requirements (e.g. duplicates are allowed, so the max should be higher, or some other constraint).

```python
# members set has 4 allowed values
validator.expect_column_values_to_have_list_members(column="programs", list_members=["A", "B", "C", "D"])

# ✅ CORRECT — max length equals len(list_members)
validator.expect_column_values_to_have_list_length_in_range(column="programs", list_length_range=[1, 4])

# ❌ WRONG — max of 10 has no basis when only 4 members are allowed
validator.expect_column_values_to_have_list_length_in_range(column="programs", list_length_range=[1, 10])
```

The same reasoning applies when both constraints are expressed inside a JSON schema for a nested
column: `maxItems` should equal the number of allowed values in the corresponding `enum`/member
list (see the "Custom list expectations do NOT work on nested columns" warning in Step 3 for why
nested columns use JSON schema instead of the custom expectations directly).

