### ⚠️ GX dedups expectations by domain (`type` + column + `row_condition`)

GX identifies each expectation by its **`(expectation_type + domain kwargs)`**, where *domain kwargs*
= the **column** plus **`row_condition`/`condition_parser`** — **not** the full kwargs. Value kwargs
(`json_schema`, `value_set`, `min_value`, a custom `target_field`, etc.) are **not** part of the
identity. Adding a second expectation with the same `(type, column, row_condition)` **overwrites** the
first on load/validation — silently, with no error. Consequences:

- **Same column + same type + no (or identical) `row_condition` → they collide.** Two
  `match_json_schema` (or two `be_in_set`, or two custom `nested_object_*`) on one column keep only the
  last. Two custom nested-object expectations that differ only by `target_field` also collide —
  `target_field` is a value kwarg, not a domain kwarg. To put several checks on one column they must
  differ in **type** or in **`row_condition`**.
- **Different `row_condition` → different domain → both are kept.** This is the supported way to attach
  multiple same-type expectations to one column, each validating a disjoint row subset (e.g. one schema
  where `cluster == "X"`, another where `cluster != "X"`). *Caveat:* a `row_condition` that hardcodes
  data values (cluster names, categories) is brittle — if those values drift, the condition silently
  mis-targets and the check stops doing what you think. Prefer a single condition-free expectation
  (e.g. a `oneOf` schema covering all valid states) unless the per-subset precision is worth the
  coupling.
- **When verifying or testing an expectation, reproduce its FULL kwargs — never drop
  `row_condition`/`condition_parser`.** Dropping the condition changes the domain, so an ad-hoc test
  can falsely show a collision (or a false pass/fail) the real suite doesn't have. Mirror the exact
  `row_condition` when checking behavior or dedup.
- **After regenerating, count the surviving expectations per column** and confirm it matches intent — a
  collision surfaces as a *lower-than-expected count* / a missing check, not an error.

---

### ⚠️ Custom list expectations do NOT work on nested columns

`expect_column_values_to_have_list_length_in_range`, `expect_column_values_to_have_list_members_of_type`, and `expect_column_values_to_have_unique_list_members` all check `isinstance(cell, list)` internally. By the time GX runs, any column listed in `gx_nested_columns` has already been converted to a JSON **string** by `convert_nested_columns_to_json`, so these expectations will always fail silently (returning `False` for every row).

**For list/array columns declared in `gx_nested_columns`, use `expect_column_values_to_match_json_schema` exclusively.** Encode length bounds with `minItems`/`maxItems` inside the schema, type constraints with the `"type"` and `"items"` keywords, and uniqueness with `"uniqueItems": true` (the schema-native parallel to `expect_column_values_to_have_unique_list_members` — and unlike that custom expectation, `uniqueItems` also works on arrays of objects, not just scalars, since it does deep equality rather than a `set()`-based check):

```python
# ❌ WRONG — list expectations fail on JSON-stringified nested columns
validator.expect_column_values_to_have_list_length_in_range(column="result_order", list_length_range=[2, 4])
validator.expect_column_values_to_have_list_members_of_type(column="result_order", member_type="str")
validator.expect_column_values_to_have_unique_list_members(column="result_order")

# ✅ CORRECT — JSON schema works on the string representation
validator.expect_column_values_to_match_json_schema(
    "result_order",
    json_schema={
        "type": "array",
        "minItems": 2,
        "maxItems": 4,
        "uniqueItems": True,
        "items": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
    },
)

# ✅ CORRECT — for an array of objects with required properties
validator.expect_column_values_to_match_json_schema(
    "data",
    json_schema={
        "type": "array",
        "minItems": 1,
        "maxItems": 100,
        "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "field_a": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
                "field_b": {"type": "number"},
            },
            "required": ["field_a", "field_b"],
            "additionalProperties": False,
        },
    },
)
```

### Regex syntax: Python vs JSON Schema

Use the correct regex flavor depending on where the regex is written:

| Context | Flavor | Notes |
|---------|--------|-------|
| Python `.py` files (e.g. `expect_column_values_to_match_regex`) | **Python `re` / RE2** | Raw strings (`r"..."`) preferred; no lookbehind of variable length |
| JSON schema files (`json_schemas/**/*.json`) via `"pattern"` key | **ECMAScript / JavaScript** | No named groups `(?P<name>...)`, no `\A`/`\Z` anchors — use `^`/`$` instead |

```python
# ✅ Python (re/RE2) — in .py or .ipynb
validator.expect_column_values_to_match_regex("col", regex=r"^\d{6}$")
validator.expect_column_values_to_match_regex("url", regex=r"^https://")
```

```json
// ✅ ECMAScript — in json_schemas/**/*.json
{ "pattern": "^\\d{6}$" }
{ "pattern": "^https://" }
```

```json
// ❌ WRONG in JSON schema — Python-only syntax
{ "pattern": "(?P<name>\\w+)" }   // named groups not supported in ECMAScript
{ "pattern": "\\Ahttps://" }      // \A anchor not supported — use ^ instead
```

