## Step 4 — Generate the Expectations Suite

**Option A is the default for every dataset.** Only `rna_de_aggregate` and `rna_de_individual`
currently use Option B, because their processed output is too large (2+ GB) for `pd.read_json()` to
handle interactively. Do not use Option B for any other dataset unless Option A is first
attempted and found genuinely untenable due to file size — file size is the *only* justification
for Option B, not convenience.

### Option A — Update & run the notebook (standard, for small/medium datasets)

**The `.ipynb` notebook in `gx_suite_definitions/` is the source of truth for every Option A
dataset, not just a historical artifact.** When you add, remove, or edit any expectation for an
Option A dataset, you MUST make the edit in its notebook — both the `import` in Cell 0
(if it's a custom expectation not already imported) and the actual `validator.expect_...()` call in
the cell for that column — then regenerate the suite by running the notebook. **Do not hand-edit
the generated `src/agoradatatools/great_expectations/gx/expectations/<transform>.json` directly for
an Option A dataset.** Editing it by hand leaves the notebook out of sync with the generated suite,
so the next person who runs the notebook will silently regenerate a suite that's missing your
change.

With your environment set up per the prerequisite above (editable `pip install -e ".[dev]"`),
generate the suite by executing the notebook; after successful execution, immediately strip its outputs:

```bash
jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --inplace --execute gx_suite_definitions/<transform>.ipynb
# nbconvert --execute bakes cell outputs into the .ipynb. Strip them IMMEDIATELY — as part of
# generating the suite, right after execution — so the notebook stays output-free like every other
# suite notebook. Scope pre-commit to just the nbstripout hook and this file:
pre-commit run nbstripout --files gx_suite_definitions/<transform>.ipynb
```

**Strip via `pre-commit`, not at commit time.** Do the strip as part of generation, immediately after
execution — not deferred to commit, since commits could be made by someone (the user) other than whoever (AI)
generated the suite. Go through `pre-commit`, not `nbstripout` directly: `nbstripout` isn't installed
as a standalone command, whereas `pre-commit` provisions the pinned version (`kynan/nbstripout 0.6.1`
in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`). Use `pre-commit run nbstripout --files <notebook>` for one notebook, or
`pre-commit run --all-files` for the full pre-push sweep (per the README). Do not install a git commit
hook — the README's workflow is manual invocation.

### Option B — Write the JSON directly (only for `rna_de_aggregate` / `rna_de_individual` today)

For transforms whose output JSON is very large (2+ GB), `pd.read_json()` in the notebook can take 30–60+ minutes and the `nbconvert` run becomes impractical. In this case, write `src/agoradatatools/great_expectations/gx/expectations/<transform>.json` directly — it has a well-defined format and produces the identical result.

> **Before proceeding with Option B:** Ask the user for a suitable data source to use for verification. Do NOT assume that a file in `staging/` is available, current, or matches the transform being validated — the staging directory is gitignored and its contents vary per developer. Good questions to ask:
> - "Do you have a local copy of the processed output available?"
> - "What is the Synapse ID of the processed output file? I can download a copy for verification."
> - "Do you have a smaller sample or subset of the output I can use to smoke-test the suite?"
>
> Once a data source is confirmed, load only a small sample (e.g. 500 records) for verification — do not attempt to load the full large file.

The format mirrors existing suites (e.g. `rnaseq_differential_expression.json`):

```json
{
  "data_asset_type": null,
  "expectation_suite_name": "<transform>",
  "expectations": [
    {
      "expectation_type": "expect_column_values_to_be_of_type",
      "kwargs": { "column": "my_col", "type_": "str" },
      "meta": {}
    },
    {
      "expectation_type": "expect_column_values_to_match_json_schema",
      "kwargs": {
        "column": "my_nested_col",
        "json_schema": {
          "$id": "https://github.com/Sage-Bionetworks/agora-data-tools/src/agoradatatools/great_expectations/gx/json_schemas/<transform>/<schema>.json",
          "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2019-09/schema",
          "type": "object",
          "properties": { "link_url": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1 } },
          "required": ["link_url"],
          "additionalProperties": false
        }
      },
      "meta": {}
    }
  ],
  "ge_cloud_id": null,
  "meta": { "great_expectations_version": "0.18.1" }
}
```

When hand-writing an inline `json_schema` here, include the `$schema`/`$id` header (as shown
above) so the suite matches what a notebook would generate — see Step 2.

After writing the file directly, **always verify the suite passes** by running GX on a small sample in a standalone script. This catches any expectation mismatches without loading the full file.

```python
import json, pandas as pd, great_expectations as gx
from agoradatatools.gx import GreatExpectationsRunner

# get_context FIRST so plugins dir is on sys.path
context = gx.get_context(project_root_dir='src/agoradatatools/great_expectations')
# Import any custom expectations used in the suite AFTER get_context
from expectations.expect_column_values_to_have_list_length_in_range import ExpectColumnValuesToHaveListLengthInRange

# Use a path confirmed with the user — do not assume staging/ is available
with open('<confirmed_data_path>') as f:
    sample = json.load(f)[:500]   # 500 records is enough for a smoke test

df = pd.DataFrame(sample)
df = GreatExpectationsRunner.convert_nested_columns_to_json(df, ["col_a", "col_b"])

validator = context.sources.pandas_default.read_dataframe(df)
validator.expectation_suite_name = '<transform>'

checkpoint = context.add_or_update_checkpoint(name='agora-test-checkpoint', validator=validator)
result = checkpoint.run()
results = result.list_validation_results()[0]
print(results.statistics)
# Inspect any failures:
for r in results.results:
    if not r.success:
        print('FAILED:', r.expectation_config.expectation_type, r.expectation_config.kwargs.get('column'))
```

