## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'expectations'` | Move `gx.get_context()` before custom imports in Cell 0 |
| `InvalidExpectationConfigurationError: Invalid positional argument` | Use `column=` keyword arg for all custom expectations |
| Suite not found at runtime | Run `pip install .` after generating the `.json` file |
| Pre-commit hook modifies files on commit | Re-stage modified files and commit again |
| A second expectation on a column silently disappears / a suite has fewer expectations than authored | GX dedups by `(expectation_type + column + row_condition/condition_parser)`; two same-type, same-column expectations with no/identical `row_condition` collide (last wins). Differentiate by type or `row_condition`, or combine into one schema. After regenerating, count expectations per column — see "GX dedups expectations by domain" in Step 3 |
| Ad-hoc test shows a collision / wrong pass-fail the real suite doesn't have | You dropped `row_condition`/`condition_parser` when reproducing the expectation, changing its domain. Always mirror the FULL kwargs when verifying behavior or dedup |
| Regex fails on a zero-padded string column (e.g. `jax_id`) | `pd.read_json()` converts `"004807"` → `4807` (int); validate as `int`, not `str` |
| Regex fails on a nullable column | Use `row_condition="col.notnull()"` + `condition_parser="pandas"` to skip null rows |
| Regex pattern doesn't match actual data | Always inspect the staging file directly — don't rely on the transform source code alone |
| Wrong regex flavor used | Use Python `re`/RE2 syntax in `.py`/`.ipynb` files; use ECMAScript syntax in `json_schemas/**/*.json` `"pattern"` fields |
| `expect_column_values_to_have_list_length_in_range` / `expect_column_values_to_have_list_members_of_type` / `expect_column_values_to_have_unique_list_members` always fail on nested columns | These expectations check `isinstance(cell, list)` and fail after `convert_nested_columns_to_json` converts the column to a JSON string. Use `expect_column_values_to_match_json_schema` with `minItems`/`maxItems`, `"items": {"type": ...}`, and `"uniqueItems": true` instead |
| `nbconvert` execution hangs or takes 60+ minutes | The staging file is too large for `pd.read_json()` to process interactively. Switch to Option B in Step 4: write the JSON directly, then ask the user for a confirmed data source (local file or Synapse ID) to verify the suite on a 500-record sample — do not assume `staging/` is available or up-to-date |
| New JSON schema missing `$schema` / `$id` (in a file **or** inlined in a suite) | Every GX JSON schema must carry `$schema` (draft 2019-09) and a GitHub repo-path `$id`, whether it lives in a standalone file or is embedded inline in an `expect_column_values_to_match_json_schema` kwarg — see Step 2 |
| GX value set silently drifts from the ui_config filter dropdown | Several GX `expect_column_values_to_be_in_set` / `expect_column_values_to_have_list_members` lists (and nested-column `enum`s) mirror filter-value vectors in the Agora (`AG-2005_ui_config.rmd`) and model_ad (`MG-402_Create ui_config source file.rmd`) ui_config notebooks (see "Keeping Related Constraints in Sync" → "External sync"). When you change one, prompt the user to update the parallel list in the R notebook |
| `list_length_in_range` max is arbitrary/padded on a column that also has `list_members` | Set the range's max to `len(list_members)` (or the `enum` count in a JSON schema) unless the user explicitly requires otherwise — see "Keeping Related Constraints in Sync" → "Internal sync" |
| Suite `.json` edited by hand for an Option A dataset, notebook left unchanged | Only `rna_de_aggregate`/`rna_de_individual` use Option B. For every other dataset, edit the `.ipynb` (Cell 0 import + the column's `validator.expect_...()` call) and regenerate via `nbconvert` — see Step 4 |
| Notebook left with execution outputs after `nbconvert --execute` | Strip immediately after executing — as part of generation, not at commit time — with `pre-commit run nbstripout --files <notebook>`. All suite notebooks are kept output-free. `nbstripout` isn't a standalone command, so go through `pre-commit`; don't install a git commit hook — see Step 4 |
